<!-- doc/src/sgml/encrypt-modules.sgml -->

<chapter id="encrypt-modules">
 <title>Encryption Modules</title>
 <indexterm zone="encrypt-modules">
  <primary>Encryption Modules</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <para>
  PostgreSQL provides infrastructure to create custom modules for encrypting
  files produced by <command>pg_basebackup</command> and streaming
  replication.  While encrypting via a shell command (i.e.,
  <xref linkend="guc-encrypt-command"/>) is much simpler, a custom encryption
  module will often be considerably more robust and performant.
 </para>

 <para>
  When a custom <xref linkend="guc-encrypt-library"/> is configured, PostgreSQL
  will submit files to the module for encryption.  It is ultimately up to the
  module to decide what to do with each file.
 </para>

 <para>
  Encryption modules must at least consist of an initialization function
  (see <xref linkend="encrypt-module-init"/>) and the required callbacks (see
  <xref linkend="encrypt-module-callbacks"/>).  However, encryption modules
  are also permitted to do much more (e.g., declare GUCs).
 </para>

 <sect1 id="encrypt-module-init">
  <title>Initialization Functions</title>
  <indexterm zone="encrypt-module-init">
   <primary>_PG_encrypt_module_init</primary>
  </indexterm>
  <para>
   An encryption library is loaded by dynamically loading a shared library
   with the <xref linkend="guc-encrypt-library"/>'s name as the library base
   name.  The normal library search path is used to locate the library.  To
   provide the required encryption module callbacks and to indicate that the
   library is actually an encryption module, it needs to provide a function
   named <function>_PG_encrypt_module_init</function>.  The result of the
   function must be a pointer to a struct of type
   <structname>EncryptModuleCallbacks</structname>, which contains everything
   that the core code needs to know to make use of the encryption module.
   The return value needs to be of server lifetime, which is typically
   achieved by defining it as a <literal>static const</literal> variable in
   global scope.

<programlisting>
typedef struct EncryptModuleCallbacks
{
    EncryptStartupCB startup_cb;
    EncryptCheckConfiguredCB check_configured_cb;
    EncryptFileCB encrypt_file_cb;
    DecryptFileCB decrypt_file_cb;
    EncryptShutdownCB shutdown_cb;
} EncryptModuleCallbacks;
typedef const EncryptModuleCallbacks *(*EncryptModuleInit) (void);
</programlisting>

   Only the <function>encrypt_file_cb</function> callback is required.  The
   others are optional.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="encrypt-module-callbacks">
  <title>Encryption Module Callbacks</title>
  <para>
   The encryption callbacks define the actual encryption behavior of the
   module.  The server will call them as required to process each individual
   file.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="encrypt-module-startup">
   <title>Startup Callback</title>
   <para>
    The <function>startup_cb</function> callback is called shortly after the
    module is loaded.  This callback can be used to perform any additional
    initialization required.  If the encryption module has any state, it can
    use <structfield>state->private_data</structfield> to store it.

<programlisting>
typedef void (*EncryptStartupCB) (EncryptModuleState *state);
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="encrypt-module-check">
   <title>Check Callback</title>
   <para>
    The <function>check_configured_cb</function> callback is called to
    determine whether the module is fully configured and ready to accept
    files (e.g., its configuration parameters are set to valid values).  If no
    <function>check_configured_cb</function> is defined, the server always
    assumes the module is configured.

<programlisting>
typedef bool (*EncryptCheckConfiguredCB) (EncryptModuleState *state);
</programlisting>

    If <literal>true</literal> is returned, the server will proceed with
    encrypting the file by calling the <function>encrypt_file_cb</function>
    callback.  If <literal>false</literal> is returned, encryption will not
    proceed, and the server will emit a warning message to the log.
   </para>

   <note>
    <para>
     When returning <literal>false</literal>, it may be useful to append some
     additional information to the generic warning message.  To do that,
     provide a message to the <function>enc_module_check_errdetail</function>
     macro before returning <literal>false</literal>.  Like
     <function>errdetail()</function>, this macro accepts a format string
     followed by an optional list of arguments.  The resulting string will be
     emitted as the <literal>DETAIL</literal> line of the warning message.
    </para>
   </note>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="encrypt-module-encrypt">
   <title>Encrypt Callback</title>
   <para>
    The <function>encrypt_file_cb</function> callback is called to encrypt a
    single file.

<programlisting>
typedef bool (*EncryptFileCB) (EncryptModuleState *state, const char *file, const char *path);
</programlisting>

    If <literal>true</literal> is returned, the server proceeds as if the file
    was successfully encrypted.  If <literal>false</literal> is returned or an
    error is thrown, the server will keep the original file and retry
    encryption later.
    <replaceable>file</replaceable> will contain just the file name of the
    file to encrypt, while <replaceable>path</replaceable> contains the full
    path of the file (including the file name).
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="encrypt-module-decrypt">
   <title>Decrypt Callback</title>
   <para>
    The <function>decrypt_file_cb</function> callback is called to decrypt a
    single file.

<programlisting>
typedef bool (*DecryptFileCB) (EncryptModuleState *state, const char *file, const char *path);
</programlisting>

    If <literal>true</literal> is returned, the server proceeds as if the file
    was successfully decrypted.  If <literal>false</literal> is returned or an
    error is thrown, the server will keep the original file and retry
    decryption later.
   </para>
  </sect2>

  <sect2 id="encrypt-module-shutdown">
   <title>Shutdown Callback</title>
   <para>
    The <function>shutdown_cb</function> callback is called when the
    encryption module is unloaded (e.g., after an error) or the value of
    <xref linkend="guc-encrypt-library"/> changes.  If no
    <function>shutdown_cb</function> is defined, no special action is taken in
    these situations.  If the encryption module has any state, this callback
    should free it to avoid leaks.

<programlisting>
typedef void (*EncryptShutdownCB) (EncryptModuleState *state);
</programlisting>
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>
</chapter>
